<BaseLayout title="Pusher & Diverter Mechanisms for Conveyors | PackagingConveyor.com" description="Conveyor pushers and diverters: pneumatic pushers, servo pushers, blade diverters, pop-up transfers, and ARB diverters for packaging line product routing." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-functions/pusher-diverter/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema, faqSchema]}>
<h1>Pusher &amp; Diverter Mechanisms</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Conveyor pushers and diverters route products off a main conveyor line onto spur conveyors, into lanes, or into reject bins. Pushers apply a lateral force perpendicular to the conveyor direction using pneumatic cylinders or servo actuators. Diverters redirect product at an angle using pivoting blades, pop-up wheels, or activated roller belts. The right choice depends on product weight and fragility, the time window between products, and how many destinations the line needs.</p>
<h2 id="diff-heading">Pushers vs. Diverters</h2>
<p><strong>Pushers</strong> move product sideways, perpendicular to the conveyor travel direction. A push plate or blade extends from one side of the conveyor, contacts the product, and pushes it across the belt to the opposite side where a spur conveyor, chute, or bin receives it. The product's forward motion on the conveyor combines with the lateral push force, creating a diagonal trajectory. Pushers are compact, quick to cycle, and handle lightweight to medium products.</p>
<p><strong>Diverters</strong> redirect product at an angle without applying a perpendicular force. A pivoting blade, set of angled pop-up wheels, or activated roller belt surface changes the product's direction using the product's existing forward momentum. The product follows a smooth, angled path from the main line to the spur line. Diverters handle heavier products more gently than pushers because they redirect rather than shove.</p>
<p>The choice between pusher and diverter depends on product weight, fragility, line speed, and the number of divert destinations. For <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject applications</a>, pushers are standard because reject products do not require gentle handling. For routing products to multiple packing stations or lane destinations, diverters provide gentler handling and support more spur lines per mechanism.</p>
<h2 id="pusher-heading">Pusher Mechanisms</h2>
<h3>Pneumatic Pushers</h3>
<p>Pneumatic pushers use compressed air cylinders to extend and retract a push plate. The cylinder fires on a PLC signal, the push plate contacts the product, pushes it across the conveyor, and retracts. Cycle time (extend plus retract) depends on stroke length, bore, air pressure, and product weight. The stroke must carry the product's far edge past the opposite side of the conveyor so it clears the belt completely.</p>
<p>Pneumatic pushers require plant compressed air and a solenoid valve. Air consumption increases with cycle rate, bore, and stroke length, so a fast reject pusher with a long stroke uses a significant volume of air. Flow control valves on the cylinder adjust push speed, too fast damages product, too slow misses the timing window.</p>
<p>Pneumatic pushers suit most <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject system</a> applications, lane diverting for cases and cartons, and product routing at moderate speeds. They are the lowest-cost pusher option and the simplest to maintain. Replacement cylinders and seals are commodity items.</p>
<h3>Pneumatic Reject Cylinders</h3>
<p>A reject cylinder is a pneumatic pusher dedicated to knocking a failed product off the line into a bin or reject lane. Most product reject systems on packaging lines use one. Getting it right depends on five items:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stroke.</strong> The push plate must carry the product far enough to clear the conveyor and any guide rail on the far side. Too short a stroke leaves product hanging on the edge.</li>
<li><strong>Cushioning.</strong> End-of-stroke cushions (adjustable air cushions or elastic bumpers) slow the piston before it hits the end cap. Cushioning reduces shock, noise, and wear on the cylinder and mounting.</li>
<li><strong>Guided rods.</strong> A push plate takes side load and tends to rotate. Guided cylinders, or a standard cylinder with external guide rods, keep the plate square and protect the piston rod seal.</li>
<li><strong>Valve and air supply.</strong> A fast-acting solenoid valve mounted close to the cylinder shortens response time. The air supply must hold pressure during repeated firing, and flow controls set extend and retract speed.</li>
<li><strong>Sensor timing.</strong> A sensor or inspection device detects the bad product, and the controller fires the cylinder after a delay based on conveyor speed and the distance to the pusher. An encoder on the conveyor keeps the timing correct when speed changes. A confirmation sensor after the reject point verifies the product actually left the line.</li>
</ul>
<p>See <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject systems</a> for how reject cylinders compare with air blast, diverter arm, and retracting belt rejects.</p>
<h3>Servo Pushers</h3>
<p>Servo pushers use an electric servo motor driving a linear actuator (ball screw, belt drive, or rack-and-pinion) to extend and retract the push plate. Servo control provides programmable stroke length, push speed, acceleration profile, and position accuracy. Moves can be tuned for speed or gentleness, and the servo can adjust stroke length on the fly for different product sizes without mechanical changes.</p>
<p>Servo pushers cost more than pneumatic pushers because of the servo motor, drive, and actuator, but they eliminate compressed air requirements and provide superior control for high-speed, high-precision applications. The programmable stroke length allows one pusher to handle multiple product sizes by selecting a recipe in the PLC, no manual adjustment between product changeovers.</p>
<p>Servo pushers are preferred for high-speed <a href="/conveyor-functions/slug-loading/">slug loading</a> applications where the pusher must position product precisely for case packer infeed, and for multi-product lines where changeover time matters. The servo drive also provides diagnostic data (motor current, position error) that aids predictive <a href="/resources/conveyor-maintenance/">maintenance</a>.</p>
<h2 id="diverter-heading">Diverter Mechanisms</h2>
<h3>Blade Diverters</h3>
<p>Blade diverters use a pivoting arm (blade) that swings into the product path at an angle. The blade redirects product off the main conveyor onto a spur conveyor. When not diverting, the blade parks flush with the conveyor side rail, allowing product to pass straight. Blade diverters handle medium to heavy products such as cases, totes, and bundles.</p>
<p>The blade pivot point is upstream, and the blade tip reaches across the conveyor at the divert angle. Product contacts the blade face and slides along it onto the spur line. The blade surface is typically UHMW polyethylene or stainless steel, low friction to prevent product stalling against the blade. Some blade diverters add a powered belt on the blade face to keep product moving.</p>
<p>Blade diverters require more conveyor length than pushers because the blade extends at an angle across the belt. By geometry, a blade set at 45 degrees across a 24-inch-wide conveyor occupies 24 inches of conveyor length, and a shallower angle occupies more. Product must clear the blade before the next product arrives. At high speeds with tight product spacing, the blade must cycle quickly or multiple consecutive products divert together.</p>
<h3>Pop-Up Wheel &amp; Roller Diverters</h3>
<p>Pop-up wheel diverters use rows of angled wheels or rollers that rise through the conveyor surface (between rollers or through belt slots) to redirect product. When retracted, wheels sit below the conveyor surface and product travels straight. When activated, wheels pop up above the surface, contact the product bottom, and steer it at the wheel angle onto the spur line.</p>
<p>Pop-up diverters provide smooth, gentle diverting with no lateral impact force. They suit flat-bottom products, cases, cartons, trays, totes, that ride stably on the angled wheels. They cannot handle products with irregular bottoms or products too light to engage the wheel friction. <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">Transfer conveyors</a> use pop-up roller and wheel mechanisms for right-angle product routing.</p>
<h3>Activated Roller Belt (ARB)</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/activated-roller-belt/">Activated roller belt (ARB)</a> technology from Intralox embeds angled rollers in a modular plastic belt. When the rollers contact an activation surface beneath the belt, they rotate and move product sideways while the belt keeps moving forward. <a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-400/30-degree-angled-rollertm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Intralox states</a> that when the belt rollers are in motion, product moves faster than the belt, and when they do not rotate, product travels at belt speed. Activation can be fixed, so every product is steered the same way, or switched by actuators that engage and release the activation surface on command. Switched activation is what lets an ARB sorter divert only selected products, and it needs product tracking in the controls. The actuation hardware differs by equipment design, so ask the supplier how it is switched and how quickly it responds.</p>
<p>Because the belt never stops or indexes, ARB diverts at conveyor speed. <a href="https://www.intralox.com/products/arb-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Intralox lists</a> 90-degree transfers, aligning, gapping, induction, merging, singulating, sorting, and switching among ARB functions. ARB suits flat-bottomed products such as cartons, cases, and totes, and is used in <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">laning applications</a>.</p>
<h3>Sliding Shoe Sorter</h3>
<p>Sliding shoe sorters use an array of small sliding shoes mounted on a slat conveyor surface. Individual shoes shift diagonally to push product off the side at specific divert points. Each shoe is independently controlled, allowing products to divert to different spur lines along the sorter length. Sliding shoe sorters reach the highest sort rates of the mechanisms on this page and can route to many spur destinations along one sorter.</p>
<p>Sliding shoe sorters are primarily used in distribution and fulfillment rather than packaging lines, but they appear in high-volume packaging operations that route finished cases to multiple palletizing stations or shipping lanes. See <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/sortation/">sortation systems</a> for how shoe sorters compare with other sorter types. See the <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems guide</a> for sortation system architecture.</p>
<h2 id="compliant-rigid">Compliant vs. Rigid Diverters</h2>
<p>A rigid diverter uses a hard arm or paddle (steel, aluminum, or a hard plastic wear face) that holds a fixed line and forces product to follow it. A compliant diverter uses a face that gives on contact, such as a padded or flexible paddle, a brush, or a powered belt on the arm face, so it absorbs the hit and conforms to the product instead of bouncing it. Choose rigid for heavy, robust cases where exact routing matters most. Choose compliant for light, tall, fragile, or irregular packages that tip, dent, or jam against a hard arm.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Factor</th>
<th>Rigid diverter arm or paddle</th>
<th>Compliant diverter arm or paddle</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>How it works</td>
<td>Hard face swings or extends into the product path and holds a fixed angle. Product slides along the face.</td>
<td>Flexible, padded, brush, or belt-covered face deflects on contact and spreads the force over the product side.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Product types</td>
<td>Cases, totes, shrink bundles, and other heavy or square products that resist impact</td>
<td>Bottles, pouches, bags, light cartons, trays, and products with irregular or soft sides</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Speed and impact behavior</td>
<td>Precise, repeatable path. Impact is transmitted directly to the product, so higher speeds raise the risk of tipping, rebound, or damage.</td>
<td>Softer hit and less rebound. The path is less exact because the face gives, so very heavy products may push through or drift.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Wear items</td>
<td>Low-friction wear strip on the face, pivot bushings, actuator</td>
<td>Pads, brushes, flexible paddles, or face belt that wear and need periodic replacement, plus pivot and actuator</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Ask the supplier to run your actual packages, including the lightest and tallest SKUs, at line speed before choosing a face type.</p>
