<h2 id="selection-heading">Selecting a Pusher or Diverter</h2>
<p>Match the mechanism to these parameters:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Product weight and fragility.</strong> Lightweight, durable products (cartons, pouches) tolerate pusher impact. Heavy or fragile products (glass bottles, trays) need diverters that redirect without impact. Very heavy products are usually better served by blade or pop-up diverters than by pushers.</li>
<li><strong>Line speed and product spacing.</strong> Calculate the available time between products: Time (ms) = (Product gap in inches / Belt speed in inches per second) x 1000. The mechanism cycle time must be shorter than this window. At 200 FPM (40 inches per second) with 6-inch gaps, each window is 150 ms. Compare that window with the supplier's rated cycle time.</li>
<li><strong>Number of divert destinations.</strong> Single-destination diverting uses pushers or blade diverters. Multi-destination routing uses sliding shoe sorters, ARB sorters, or sequential blade diverters.</li>
<li><strong>Compressed air availability.</strong> Pneumatic pushers and some blade diverters require compressed air. Servo pushers run on electric power only. ARB actuators and pop-up wheel lifts can be pneumatic or electric depending on the design, so confirm with the supplier. Facilities without compressed air infrastructure should specify electric mechanisms.</li>
<li><strong>Changeover frequency.</strong> Lines running multiple product sizes benefit from servo pushers with programmable stroke length. Pneumatic pushers require manual stroke adjustment between product sizes. Blade diverters and ARB mechanisms handle multiple product sizes without adjustment.</li>
<li><strong>Guarding.</strong> A push plate closing against a frame or guide rail is a pinch point. <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.212" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 1910.212</a> requires guarding of hazards such as points of operation and ingoing nip points, and <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a> covers conveyor safety. Specify guards or light curtains with the mechanism.</li>
</ol>
